\begin{definition}[Random oracle combiner, informal; Dodis, Ferguson, Goldin, Hall, and Pietrzak~\cite{DFGHP23}]\label{def:roc}
Let $C^{h_1,h_2}_{\mathcal Z}\colon\{0,1\}^m\to\{0,1\}^n$ take a message $M$, a salt $\mathcal Z=(\mathcal Z_1,\mathcal Z_2)$, and oracle access to $h_1,h_2$. Say $C$ is a secure random oracle combiner if, for every oracle circuit $g$, both $C^{\mathcal H,g^{\mathcal H}}_{\mathcal Z}$ and $C^{g^{\mathcal H},\mathcal H}_{\mathcal Z}$ are indifferentiable, in the sense of Maurer, Renner, and Holenstein, from a fresh random oracle $\mathcal G\colon\{0,1\}^m\to\{0,1\}^n$, over the random choice of $\mathcal Z$, against every polynomial-query distinguisher.
\end{definition}

candidate.formal_statement_latex:
Fix a security parameter $\lambda$. Say a random oracle combiner $C^{h_1,h_2}_{\mathcal Z_1,\mathcal Z_2}\colon\{0,1\}^m\to\{0,1\}^n$, secure per Definition~\ref{def:roc}, has \emph{message-independent salts and calls} if
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] $|\mathcal Z_1|,|\mathcal Z_2| = O(\lambda)$, independent of $m=|M|$; and
\item[(b)] $C$ makes only $O(1)$ calls to $h_1^*$ and $h_2^*$ (the Merkle-Damgard extensions of $h_1,h_2$), independent of $m$.
\end{itemize}
Does there exist a secure random oracle combiner with message-independent salts and calls? Conversely, show that no secure random oracle combiner can satisfy both (a) and (b) simultaneously; in that case, determine the optimal trade-off between the salt length $|\mathcal Z_1|,|\mathcal Z_2|$ and the number of calls made to $h_1^*,h_2^*$.
